P(d, "The policy implication, if one takes the numbers seriously, is that exclusion of complex tasks is a reach problem and not a pricing problem: no pricing instrument brings in complex tasks once *δ* is below the critical value. We regard these extensions as illustrations. The complexity layer takes the stakes distribution to be the same in easy and complex tasks. If complex tasks also carry higher stakes, the exclusion in Proposition 3 compounds it.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ E
H1(d, "E. Estimation Machinery for the NHPP Regression Layer")
P(d, "The paper's theory is self-contained, and the model of Section 3 does not require estimation. For the empirical work that the model suggests, we built and validated a hierarchical Bayesian count model that estimates the pool *a* and rate *b* of paired human and AI-assisted test campaigns, with covariates for the use of AI assistance and for task complexity, and that carries posterior uncertainty to the net value of AI testing. This section documents what has been validated, using simulated data only. No estimates in the main text come from this model.")
P(d, "*Model.* For project *i* and test condition *L* ∈ {0, 1}, the expected number of faults found in interval (*t*_{*j*−1}, *t*_{*j*}] is *a*_{*iL*}(e^{−*b*_{*iL*}*t*_{*j*−1}} − e^{−*b*_{*iL*}*t*_{*j*}}), the increment of a Goel–Okumoto mean value function. We set ln *a*_{*iL*} = *β*_{0} + *β*_{llm}*L* + *β*_{cx}*x*_{*i*} + *β*_{int}*L x*_{*i*} + *u*_{*i*} and ln *b*_{*iL*} = *γ*_{0} + *γ*_{llm}*L* + *γ*_{cx}*x*_{*i*} + *v*_{*i*}, where *x*_{*i*} is task complexity and *u*, *v* are project random effects. Counts are Poisson, or negative binomial (gamma-mixed Poisson) with dispersion *α* for overdispersion. The model is implemented in PyMC with NUTS sampling. From the posterior we compute the cost-minimizing budget *T*^{*} and the net value *V* of AI-assisted testing at three complexity levels, as in Lemma 1.")
P(d, "*Calibration check.* We simulated 60 independent datasets (30 projects, 20 intervals, paired design) from fixed true parameters, refit the model to each, and recorded whether the 89 percent central posterior interval covered the true value. Coverage across the ten model parameters ranged from 0.83 to 0.97 (mean about 0.89), and for the net value *V* at complexity −1, 0, and +1 it was 0.88, 0.93, and 0.87. One of the 60 fits reported divergent transitions. The posterior probability that *V* > 0 pointed to the correct sign in 93, 65, and 87 percent of replications at the three complexity levels; the middle figure is low because the true value is close to zero there. These checks establish that the model recovers the data-generating process when it is correctly specified, and they say nothing about performance on real data.")
P(d, "*Sensitivity.* We evaluated the posterior mean of *V* and Pr(*V* > 0) over a grid of the cost of a field fault (0.5 to 20) and the cost premium of AI testing (1.0 to 3.0 times human). The sign of *V* depends on the premium, on the field-fault cost, and on task complexity, but posterior intervals are wide: at the default economics (field-fault cost 3, premium 1.3) the 89 percent interval for *V* has width 10 to 20 depending on complexity. At several grid points the posterior mean has the opposite sign to the value implied by the data-generating parameters, within the interval. Poisson and negative-binomial fits give similar posterior means (for example *V* = 4.49 versus 4.61 at average complexity), and the Poisson model has slightly narrower intervals (10.35 versus 10.67), which is the cost of ignoring overdispersion. The point of the exercise is that the economic conclusion from a single study of this size would be imprecise, which is a reason to treat the structural results of the paper as the contribution and not the numerical value of *V*. The grid is in `sensitivity_results.csv`.")

