H2(d, "F.3 Adoption of an AI coding assistant: what could and could not be done")
P(d, "We obtained a public replication package of a study of repositories that adopted an AI coding assistant (813 adopting repositories, about 78,000 candidate repositories, monthly panels of issues, commits, and code-quality metrics). We used it to (i) screen repositories for use as a difference-in-differences sample, and (ii) measure issue-label usage so that defect-related issues could be separated. Of 196 repositories that passed the screen, we collected issues through the GitHub GraphQL API for 191 (five repositories had been deleted or made private), obtaining 114,362 issues (56,677 before and 57,685 after adoption). A label was present on 71 percent of issues and a defect-like label on 32 percent. Between 53 and 69 repositories had enough defect-labeled issues both before and after adoption (at least 10 or 5, respectively) for any repository-level analysis. Among the 53, the mean monthly count of defect-labeled issues was 27.8 before and 45.9 after adoption, higher after in 37 repositories; there is no control group in this calculation and it is not evidence of any effect. The sample is too small for a controlled difference-in-differences design (Callaway and Sant'Anna 2021), and control-group labels were not collected.")
P(d, "A crude test of Proposition 2 using GitHub stars as a proxy for stakes gave U-shaped relative adoption intensity (a quadratic coefficient of 0.221 in a logistic regression, *p* < 0.001), with the largest ratios at the extremes of the popularity distribution. We do not interpret this as evidence against the model. Stars measure popularity and not the cost of faults; large projects attract more contributors, so someone is more likely to commit a configuration file; the control sample is not documented well enough to read ratios as probabilities; and low-star repositories are mostly personal projects, not firms. A proper test would need a better proxy for stakes (for example the number of dependents or production use), contributor-level adoption data, and a documented control design.")
H2(d, "F.4 A proposed identification strategy")
P(d, "A firm-level test of the model's predictions would use (i) the staggered introduction of an AI testing service across teams or firms; (ii) data on stakes, such as the cost of production incidents; (iii) estimates of the reach of the tool on the firm's own fault classes, perhaps from a pre-adoption trial; and (iv) a heterogeneity-robust difference-in-differences estimator. The model predicts that treatment effects on adoption and escaped faults vary with stakes in the single-peaked pattern of Proposition 2, and that the pattern flattens as measured reach approaches parity. The test would require firm-internal data that are not available in public sources.")

